## Current Rocket Booster Recovery first-contact task block

Goal: maximize the sole `landing_success_rate` on the frozen complete protocol.
Every overall, hard-OOD, source, and radius-bin success rate is a stratification
of the same first-contact predicate; never invent engineering/strict/standard
alternatives.

The initial fuel capacity is fixed at 7000 kg. Success is measured at
interpolated first landing-leg contact and requires lateral error <=5 m,
COM and lowest contact-leg sink speed <=1 m/s, the frozen lateral-speed,
upright, and rate limits, and remaining main fuel strictly >2%. There is no
lower impact-speed bound. Read the measured values from
`assets/baselines/results.jsonl`; do not reuse old-protocol baseline numbers.

Post-contact scored steps and damping credit are exactly zero. Never cut the
engine or command an under-supported gear-sink/drop whose apparent safety
comes from spring/damper arrest after impact. A coast segment is admissible
only if the controller brakes to the first-contact gate before any leg touches.

Preserve the multi-objective space instead of collapsing it: landing outcome,
first-contact vertical risk, 2% fuel-gate coverage, actuator saturation, and
roll robustness are distinct incubator Pareto families. Sub-5 m lateral error
and fuel beyond the 2% feasibility threshold are not alternative success tiers.

Recommended attributable integration order:
`(energy_manager + terminal_landing_manager) -> (trajectory_guidance +
constraint_governor) -> (fin_effectiveness_model + allocator_yz) ->
attitude_controller_yz -> state_disturbance_estimator -> roll_rcs_controller ->
robust_design_validation`.

The run is deliberately offline: do not browse, download papers/data, install
dependencies, or invoke a literature lookup tool. The operator-provided prior
art in `assets/literature/research_directions.md` is context, not measured
performance.
